# Line-ending policy for the whole repo. Went repo-wide 2026-09-03 (Hank).
#
# WHY, AND WHY IT WAS DEFERRED THIS LONG
# --------------------------------------
# Until now this file governed three narrow paths and said outright that the
# repo-wide decision was still open, because "a blanket rule would rewrite real
# source files" and the rewrite had to land in a window where no session was
# mid-edit. That caution was right. What changed is the measurement: of 1,436
# tracked files, only 159 blobs were CRLF and only 15 of those sat outside the
# two byte-exact archives. The rewrite is small and nameable, not repo-wide.
#
# WHAT THE ABSENCE COST, so nobody re-opens this as a matter of taste:
#   * api/sd-data.js flipped from pure CRLF (4,653 lines) to pure LF (4,828) in
#     4886658. That read as +4828/-4653 while `git diff -w` read +175/-0, and a
#     later 24-line change rebased into a ~9,000-line whole-file conflict.
#   * A pre-push hook written with CRLF was skipped SILENTLY by git on
#     2026-09-01 -- the kernel looked for "/bin/sh\r" -- while running correctly
#     by hand. That is what the .githooks rule below already exists for.
#   * On 2026-09-03 alone, three separate false alarms in one session came from
#     comparing an LF blob against a CRLF working tree: the skill-store mirror
#     diff (12 skills reported as diverged, all identical), the push gate's own
#     seed-divergence note (all 72 seed files reported as diverged on a clean
#     checkout), and a deploy-mismatch hook fire. Each looked like drift and was
#     line endings. That third instance is why this landed.
#
# THE SHAPE: explicit per-type rules, NOT `* text=auto`.
# `text=auto` guesses on unknown extensions, and this repo carries generated
# artifacts, vendored archives and a .pdf whose name contains an em dash. An
# explicit list is longer and cannot surprise anyone.
#
# `eol=lf` sets BOTH sides -- LF in the blob and LF in the working tree. The
# working-tree half is the point: a repo whose blobs are LF and whose checkout
# is CRLF is exactly the mismatch that produced all three false alarms above.
#
# THE WORKING-TREE HALF IS NOT RETROACTIVE, and saying otherwise would be the
# same kind of untrue-but-plausible claim this file exists to stop. Committing
# these rules changes blobs only. Files already on disk with CRLF keep it until
# git next writes them, and `git status` stays clean throughout, because the
# clean filter converts on the way in and the result already matches the blob.
# To make a clone's working tree match its blobs now:
#
#     git rm --cached -r -q . && git reset --hard
#
# Do that in a clone with nothing uncommitted. After it, .claude/skills/ is
# byte-identical to the ~/.claude/skills/ store it mirrors and a plain `diff`
# between the two stops reporting 12 phantom differences.
#
# ORDER MATTERS. The general rules come first; the narrow overrides at the
# bottom win, which is how the two byte-exact archives stay untouched.

# ── The frozen branch archive ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# archive/branch-lucid-ptolemy-b73vu0/ is a snapshot of a deleted branch, kept
# solely so the 901 commits under tag archive/lucid-ptolemy-b73vu0 stay
# meaningful.
#
# THE OBVIOUS RULE HERE IS `-text` AND IT IS WRONG. Verified 2026-09-03 rather
# than assumed: these blobs are ALREADY pure LF, while core.autocrlf=true means
# the working tree holds them as CRLF. `-text` tells git to store the working
# tree verbatim, so a renormalise would have BAKED CRLF INTO 150+ preservation
# blobs -- rewriting the very bytes the rule was meant to protect. `eol=lf`
# leaves the LF blobs untouched and fixes the checkout to match them.
#
# It is FIRST so the binary and .bat rules below still win inside archive/.
archive/** text eol=lf

# ── Source, docs and data: normalise ─────────────────────────────────────────
*.js    text eol=lf
*.cjs   text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.csv   text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.svg   text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.vercelignore   text eol=lf
LICENSE         text eol=lf
.graphify_version text eol=lf

# ── Windows batch files must keep CRLF ───────────────────────────────────────
# cmd.exe mis-parses a .bat written with LF -- labels and multi-line blocks in
# particular. This is the one place CRLF is correct rather than incidental.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# ── Binaries: never touched, and marked so git cannot guess wrong ────────────
*.zip  -text
*.pdf  -text
*.png  -text
*.jpg  -text
*.jpeg -text
*.gif  -text
*.ico  -text
*.woff  -text
*.woff2 -text

# ── BYTE-EXACT ARCHIVES -- these override everything above ───────────────────
# docs/skill-backups/ is a byte-exact disaster-recovery archive of 29 skills
# that exist nowhere else. `-text` disables end-of-line conversion in both
# directions, so what is committed is what is restored. A restored archive that
# differs byte-for-byte from what was backed up fails silently, and only at the
# moment you most need it to be correct.
docs/skill-backups/** -text

# docs/sources/ holds captured primary-source statute and regulation text -- the
# evidence behind SAIRNsenior's state survey.
#
# `-text` HERE IS NOT THE OBVIOUS CHOICE AND IT IS THE ONLY STABLE ONE.
# `text eol=lf` was tried first and REJECTED ON EVIDENCE, 2026-09-03: eleven of
# these files contain CR CR LF sequences from the original HTML-to-text
# extraction. Git's CRLF->LF clean filter is a single left-to-right pass, so
# `\r\r\n` becomes `\r\n` -- still CRLF. Running `git add --renormalize .` a
# SECOND time restaged all eleven again. Verified by doing exactly that: pass
# one staged 15 files, pass two staged 11 of them over. A rule that never
# reaches a fixpoint means every future renormalise churns 127,000 lines, which
# is worse than the non-determinism it was meant to end.
#
# Collapsing the double CRs fully would be a real content edit, and MANIFEST.json
# pins against it: all 32 of its `text_chars` counts match the SINGLE-pass
# normalised length. The manifest describes these files as they are.
#
# So they are frozen exactly as stored -- which is what an evidence capture
# should be anyway, and the same standard as the archive above. The stored set
# is mixed (11 CRLF, the rest LF); frozen and mixed beats churning forever.
docs/sources/** -text

# ── Executable text that MUST check out LF, restated narrowly ────────────────
# Already covered by *.sh above, kept explicit because the cost of getting these
# two wrong is a hook or script that fails in a way that does not look like a
# line-ending problem.
scripts/*.sh text eol=lf
.githooks/*  text eol=lf
